{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "Sally Goes on a Safari", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "African Animals", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 2, "type": "position"}, {"content": "scholar > Nature Around Us > Safari", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 8, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.2", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Accurately use information about people's jobs and places of work in communicative situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level.", "type": "secondary_8"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"contents": [{"content": "On a Safari", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "A safari is a trip to observe wild animals, especially in Africa. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When you talk about a safari, you use the verb \u201cto go\u201d: </br> - <span class=\"italic\">I went on a safari.</span>  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You talk about the different animals, for example: </br>  - <span class=\"italic\"> I saw many elephants </span>or<span class=\"italic\"> I didn't see any lions. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You can talk about the geographic features, for example:  </br> - <span class=\"italic\">I saw many hills. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When you talk to someone about a safari you went on, they can ask questions like:  </br> - <span class=\"italic\">Which country did you go to?</span> </br>- <span class=\"italic\"> What did you see? </span> </br>- <span class=\"italic\">Did you see any lions? </span>", "type": "text"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Comparatives and Superlatives", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use adjectives to compare two things. These are called  <span class=\"strong\">comparative</span> adjectives. You can also use adjectives to compare one item with the rest of a group. These are called <span class=\"strong\">superlative</span> adjectives.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "The lion is smaller than the tiger.", "type": "example"}, {"content": " For regular comparative adjectives, you can use the structure:  <br/> <ul><li> adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-er</span>) <span class=\"strong\">than</span><br/>  Use this when the adjective in question is a short, one-syllable word.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">small &#8594; smaller</span></li> <li>adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-ier</span>) <span class=\"strong\">than</span><br/>  Use this if the adjective ends in <span class=\"italic\">-y</span> and has two syllables.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">happy &#8594; happier</span></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">more/less</span> + adjective + <span class=\"strong\">than</span><br/> Use this if the adjective has 2 or more syllables. The adjective does not change. <br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">careful &#8594; more careful</span>, <span class=\"italic\">boring &#8594; less boring</span><br/> There are a few exceptions, however: <span class=\"italic\">simple &#8594; simpler</span>, <span class=\"italic\">narrow  &#8594; narrower</span></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">as</span> + adjective + <span class=\"strong\">as</span> <br/> Use this when comparing two things that are the same regarding the adjective in question. The adjective does not change.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">I'm as fast as you. </span></li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": " For regular superlative adjectives, you can use the structure:<br/>  <ul><li> <span class=\"strong\">the</span> adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-est</span>)<br/> Use this when the adjective in question is a short, one-syllable word.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">small &#8594; smallest</span></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">the</span> adjective (<span class=\"strong\">-iest</span>)<br/> Use this if the word ends in <span class=\"italic\">-y</span> and has two syllables.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">happy &#8594; happiest</span> <br/></li> <li><span class=\"strong\">the most/least</span> + adjective <br/>Use this if the adjective has 2 or more syllables. The adjective does not change.<br/> e.g. <span class=\"italic\">careful &#8594; most careful</span>, <span class=\"italic\">boring &#8594; most boring</span><br/> There are a few exceptions, however: <span class=\"italic\">simple &#8594; simplest</span>, <span class=\"italic\">narrow &#8594; narrowest</span></li></ul> </li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The cheetah is the fastest animal in the cat family.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "It was the best trip!", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "leopard", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a member of the cat family, with yellow fur and black spots.", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Leopards are slower than lions.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear on a safari.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "to hunt ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to chase animals and kill them", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The lion hunts at night.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "cheetah", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a member of the cat family, with long legs and black spots.", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The cheetah is the fastest land animal in the world.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "to rest", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to lie down to relax", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The lions rest in the shade.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "mane", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the long hair around the face and neck of a lion", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The lion's mane blows in the wind.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "tiger", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a large member of the cat family, with orange and black lines on its body ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  The tiger is larger than the lion.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "trunk", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the long nose of an elephant", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The elephant lifted its trunk.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02aud07", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"content": "engenn5le08ob02re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Go on the Internet and find out about all the members of the cat family.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Choose one animal.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write 5-6 sentences about the animal you chose. Describe it.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Now choose another type of cat. Compare this animal to one you wrote about.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write 3 sentences about how they are the same.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write 2 sentences about how they are different. Remember to use comparatives and superlatives.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Read your sentences aloud to the class.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I research two members of the cat family.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I compare two animals.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I read my sentences aloud.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "_data": [], "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img05.jpg", "time": "0", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img05.jpg", "time": "100", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Hey, Sally! What did you do today? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: I went on an African safari! It was the best! </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Cool. What did you see?"}, {"image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img06.jpg", "time": "10500", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: We saw lions. They hunt together at night, not like leopards. Leopards  hunt alone.  Lions are faster than leopards, too. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Not as fast as cheetahs, though. They're the fastest! </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: The lions rest in the shade during the day. They looked magnificent lying there with their manes blowing."}, {"image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img07.jpg", "time": "31000", "id": 3, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Only male lions have manes, right? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: That's right. Did you know that lions are smaller than tigers? I always thought lions were bigger. Turns out the tigers are the largest. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: I didn't. Did you see  any other animals?"}, {"image": "engenn5le08ob02re2img08.jpg", "time": "48000", "id": 4, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Sally</span>: Yes. We went on elephant rides.  It was the most amazing experience. We fed them bananas, and the coolest part was to see the elephants lift their trunks. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Vanessa</span>: Sounds like you had a blast!"}], "audiosrc": "engenn5le08ob02re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn5le08ob02re2img05.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "id": 1}], "exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can talk about travel and things to do on a trip.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about or report on actions, events or people. ", "type": "Learning Outcom